{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 6229 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "23a5fb819e0f5b26a955275c6eccb78b67dbc36040d09bbb9f2a6aae04e85d81" :edb/db-before-t 6228 :edb/db-after-t 6229 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539541 50 #inst "2026-10-24T06:43:21.280Z" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1117 #edb/ref 17592186676147 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1117 #edb/ref 17592186676155 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186048257 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186049159 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186050426 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186045416 1118 #edb/ref 17592186051014 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676121 1064 "**MTH 341 — Post-reading Questions: §§4.10.1–4.10.2**\n\nDue: 2026-10-23\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-23\nSource: [Canvas — Post-reading Questions-Section 4.10.1 and 4.10.2](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/quizzes/3193310)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are independently authored additions. Verified Math Academy links below cover the component concepts; no equivalent question matching each complete response task was verified.\n\n## Instructions\n\nAnswer all five questions. For Questions 2, 4, and 5, explain the concept in your own words and consider a geometric interpretation. Canvas specifies no time limit. The unnumbered reading definition between Questions 1 and 2 is retained below; it requests no response." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676121 1066 #uuid "0068b7a7-5bc5-5d51-a16b-9c6f70ed66b6" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676121 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676122 1080 "The vectors are linearly independent when none can be built as a linear combination of the others. Each vector contributes a direction outside the span of the others. Equivalently,\n\n$$c_1\\vec v_1+\\cdots+c_k\\vec v_k=\\vec0$$\n\nforces $c_1=\\cdots=c_k=0$.\n\nGeometrically, two nonzero vectors are independent when they are not parallel. In $\\mathbb R^3$, three independent vectors do not all lie in a plane through the origin, so together they span three-dimensional space." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676122 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676122 1083 #uuid "0f3c1dde-dfbe-5a8e-b626-72ed3fc513e7" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676156 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676123 1071 "Source question: Canvas Question 4 (question_59459089).\n\nWrite in your own words what it means for a set of vectors to be linearly independent. Is there a geometric way that you think about linear independence?\n\nYour response: {{answer}}" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676123 1072 #uuid "27c3d48f-905a-5337-8d5a-84527302be32" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676123 1074 "The test concerns linear combinations of **all** vectors in the set. For two vectors, being nonparallel is sufficient. For three or more, checking pairs is insufficient: $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,1)$ are pairwise nonparallel but the third is the sum of the first two. Independent vectors need not be perpendicular. To see the equivalence of the definitions, any nonzero coefficient in a zero combination would let you solve for its vector in terms of the others; independence rules that out." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676144 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676124 1071 "Source question: Canvas topic 11698213, Question 1. Only this mathematical question and your response to it are included.\n\nDetermine whether\n\n$$\\det(A+B)=\\det(A)+\\det(B).$$\n\nIf it is true, prove it. Otherwise, give a counterexample. If it is false in general, for what size matrices is the statement true?\n\nHere $A$ and $B$ are square matrices of the same size.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676124 1072 #uuid "3346bd32-6047-5d7d-ac4f-07c4037fdcb6" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676124 1074 "**Authored verification.** Your $2\\times2$ counterexample is correct: $A=B=I_2$ gives $A+B=2I_2$, whose determinant is $2\\cdot2-0\\cdot0=4$, while $\\det(A)+\\det(B)=1+1=2$. Your argument for $1\\times1$ matrices also proves the identity for every pair of that size.\n\nTo justify the conclusion for every larger size, take $A=B=I_n$. Then\n\n$$\\det(A+B)=\\det(2I_n)=2^n,\\qquad \\det(A)+\\det(B)=2.$$\n\nFor every integer $n\\ge2$, $2^n>2$. Thus, among positive matrix sizes, $1\\times1$ is the only size for which the equality holds for **all** pairs. It can still hold for particular larger pairs, for example when $B=0$; the conclusion concerns a universal identity.\n\nThe determinant is linear in one row at a time when all other rows are fixed. That property does not imply additivity when an entire matrix is added." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676125 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048257 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676125 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676125 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050426 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676125 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676137 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676125 1090 #uuid "37f378cf-92cf-5acd-bd6d-886d8844e320" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676152 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676126 1071 "Source question: Canvas Question 1 (question_59459085).\n\nEarlier in the course, we studied homogeneous systems of linear equations. What did we call the solution to a homogeneous system when it was unique?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676126 1072 #uuid "3b8de50a-9e1b-5007-947c-59ee13916ee8" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676126 1074 "The **trivial solution** sets every unknown equal to zero. A homogeneous system has the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$, and $A\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}$, so this solution always exists. If the solution is unique, it must be this zero solution. The name applies to the zero solution even when other solutions also exist." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676127 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676157 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676127 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676127 1077 "answer" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676127 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676157 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676127 1079 #uuid "3e3752d6-d82b-50ef-85e1-06edb537ad64" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676128 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048853 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676128 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050426 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676128 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676126 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676128 1090 #uuid "422192b9-0916-592d-9d1c-7538e435265d" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676129 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676138 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676129 1062 #uuid "4cc3363f-4e9b-5f5f-96ff-cede9e279482" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676129 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676139 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676130 1080 "No" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676130 1081 "The vectors are required to be nonzero, but their coefficients may be zero. Taking all coefficients to be zero gives a linear combination equal to the zero vector." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676130 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676130 1083 #uuid "54819f5a-4371-5522-b7a7-d2d14d302027" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049044 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049159 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049304 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049875 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676124 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676131 1090 #uuid "580348f4-de09-5b51-a23b-195c2508be96" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676148 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676132 1071 "Source question: Canvas Question 3 (question_59459088). The source's follow-up refers to the first paragraph on page 194 of the assigned reading.\n\nIs the zero vector in the span of a set of nonzero vectors?" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676132 1072 #uuid "5bc6a6ba-8ee6-5c91-bb12-b1e7c1a4b38e" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676132 1074 "Set $c_1=\\cdots=c_k=0$. Then\n\n$$0\\vec v_1+\\cdots+0\\vec v_k=\\vec0,$$\n\nwhich shows directly that the zero vector belongs to the span. This works for independent and dependent sets alike. Merely obtaining zero does not prove dependence: dependence requires a combination with **at least one nonzero coefficient**. This also explains the source's follow-up asking why; its reading reference is the first paragraph on page 194." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676133 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676133 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051014 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676133 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676151 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676133 1090 #uuid "5c9b9292-3a7f-5647-9553-fbebcd3e6493" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676134 1080 "**Your original response — Jacob Peterson (entry 43183864)**\n\nThe statement is false for matrices larger than 1x1. Given:\n\n$$A = B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$$\n\nAdding the matrices and calculating the determinants gives:\n\n$$\\det(A+B)=\\begin{vmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{vmatrix}=4$$\n\n$$\\det(A)+\\det(B)=1+1=2$$\n\nSince 4 does not equal 2, the statement is false.\n\nHowever, it is true for 1x1 matrices because the determinant is just the single entry.\n\n$$A=[a],\\quad B=[b]$$\n\n$$\\det(A+B)=a+b=\\det(A)+\\det(B)$$\n\nTherefore, the statement is always true for 1x1 matrices, but not for matrices 2x2 or larger." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676134 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676134 1083 #uuid "5d674af8-e9e7-5fe4-87a0-e4e516256097" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676135 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676136 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676135 1062 #uuid "5df767d6-0a76-5250-9255-369fa0fdac37" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676135 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676154 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676136 1062 #uuid "5fd91020-a907-5762-87a1-3cd56749ff66" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676136 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676131 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676127 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676137 1071 "Source question: Canvas Question 5 (question_59459090).\n\nWrite in your own words what it means for a set of vectors to be linearly dependent. Is there a geometric way that you think about linear dependence?\n\nYour response: {{answer}}" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676137 1072 #uuid "678a38a6-1c75-5f72-b961-1730a55eaef1" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676137 1074 "Dependence does not require two vectors to coincide or be parallel. For example, $(1,0)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,1)$ are dependent because $(1,0)+(0,1)-(1,1)=(0,0)$; none of the three pairs is parallel. The phrase **not all zero** is essential: every set has the all-zero-coefficient combination. If $c_j\\ne0$ in a zero combination, then $\\vec v_j=-\\sum_{i\\ne j}(c_i/c_j)\\vec v_i$, which expresses that vector using the others. Conversely, such an expression rearranges to a zero combination with a nonzero coefficient." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676138 1062 #uuid "74d76555-8418-5a0a-bdc3-b9e938deb4ec" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676138 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676125 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676139 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048257 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676139 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676139 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050426 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676139 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676123 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676139 1090 #uuid "7fbb2ff1-31f1-588c-b77b-6fc3980d5522" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676140 1080 "trivial solution" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676140 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676140 1083 #uuid "80e2d8d5-addc-5dbf-a8e9-483263831b5e" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676141 1080 "Yes" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676141 1081 "Choosing every coefficient equal to zero produces the zero vector, even when every vector in the given set is nonzero." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676141 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676141 1083 #uuid "84d35b71-f854-56de-890d-06aecdf972f9" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676142 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676150 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676142 1062 #uuid "84e5a4b3-9c63-5f4f-a87c-36498408d3af" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676142 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676133 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676143 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676142 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676143 1062 #uuid "90a3cd83-2b41-5914-88f1-6da79bbcac36" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676143 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676128 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676144 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676134 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676144 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676144 1077 "response" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676144 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676134 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676144 1079 #uuid "9491a36e-af35-55c9-8af6-36c6be2f9a81" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676145 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048315 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676145 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049715 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676145 1089 #edb/ref 17592186676132 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676145 1090 #uuid "a57b0e24-0682-5757-8d41-3e4136f30175" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676146 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676149 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676146 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676146 1077 "answer" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676146 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676149 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676146 1079 #uuid "a92106bf-8f05-530d-ad80-f519ff6b578e" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676135 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676136 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045461 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1045 "MTH 341 — GD-4" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1046 #edb/ref 17592186676135 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1047 #inst "2026-10-24T06:59:00.000Z" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1048 #edb/ref 17592186405206 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676147 1053 #uuid "aaeda338-1e4c-54ba-9178-229abc23f4ca" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676130 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676141 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1077 "answer" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676141 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676148 1079 #uuid "b79c1df1-d2bc-5a69-8506-52fd1b6260fc" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676149 1080 "The span is every vector I can reach by scaling the given vectors and adding them together, allowing any real coefficients. In symbols,\n\n$$\\operatorname{span}\\{\\vec v_1,\\ldots,\\vec v_k\\}=\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\\vec v_i:c_1,\\ldots,c_k\\in\\mathbb R\\right\\}.$$\n\nGeometrically, one nonzero vector spans a line through the origin. Two nonparallel vectors span a plane through the origin. Three linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$ span all of $\\mathbb R^3$. A set consisting only of the zero vector spans just the origin." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676149 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676149 1083 #uuid "bc8bbfb8-48d4-5386-8284-1be927f4bd9e" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676150 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676129 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676150 1062 #uuid "bcc58ab0-3a12-51fa-bc8c-da71e6dfca80" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676150 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676145 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186676146 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676151 1071 "Source question: Canvas Question 2 (question_59459087).\n\n**Reading definition** (unnumbered source item question_59459086, immediately before Question 2): Let $\\vec v_1,\\ldots,\\vec v_k$ and $\\vec u$ be column vectors. The vector $\\vec u$ is a **linear combination** of $\\{\\vec v_1,\\ldots,\\vec v_k\\}$ if scalars $c_1,\\ldots,c_k$ exist such that\n\n$$\n\\vec u=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\\vec v_i.\n$$\n\nWrite in your own words what the span of a set of vectors is. Is there a geometric way that you think about spans?\n\nYour response: {{answer}}" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676151 1072 #uuid "c3f03597-9831-593c-a816-b8e55677e992" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676151 1074 "A span contains **all** linear combinations, rather than just the original vectors or one particular sum. The coefficients may be positive, negative, or zero, so a single nonzero vector gives the entire line through the origin, rather than only a ray or segment. With more vectors, the geometric size depends on how many independent directions they supply, rather than just how many vectors are listed." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676152 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676140 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676152 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676152 1077 "answer" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676152 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676140 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676152 1079 #uuid "c51d110d-3b22-5031-b387-2573b2ebcef8" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676153 1058 #edb/ref 17592186676143 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676153 1062 #uuid "cb96779c-3609-5e42-bb51-0d692a8b9d60" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676153 1063 #edb/ref 17592186676121 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676154 1064 "**MTH 341 — Week 4: Determinants of Matrix Sums**\n\nDue: 2026-10-23\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDate imported: 2026-10-23\nSource: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/groups/750466/discussion_topics/11698213)\nResponse source: Jacob Peterson, entry 43183864, posted 2026-10-23 at 11:16 p.m.\n\nYour original response is preserved in the answer reveal, with equations transcribed into LaTeX. The verification feedback is an independently authored addition.\n\n## Instructions\n\nDetermine whether the proposed determinant identity is true. Prove it if true; otherwise give a counterexample and identify the matrix sizes for which it holds as an identity." 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676154 1066 #uuid "dce07c4a-a299-5620-8900-cf0cbf5c657f" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676154 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676129 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676138 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676142 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676143 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676150 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1043 #edb/ref 17592186676153 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045461 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1045 "MTH 341 — PRQ-9" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1046 #edb/ref 17592186676153 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1047 #inst "2026-10-24T06:59:00.000Z" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1048 #edb/ref 17592186405206 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676155 1053 #uuid "e8ac800a-5e66-5a8d-ab18-d1b54796af05" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676156 1075 #edb/ref 17592186676122 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676156 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676156 1077 "answer" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676156 1078 #edb/ref 17592186676122 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676156 1079 #uuid "f184df82-a1a1-5592-b05a-04818bc5b5be" 13194139539541 17592186502323 true] [17592186676157 1080 "A set is linearly dependent when at least one vector can be built as a linear combination of the others. That vector is redundant: removing it does not change the span. Equivalently, there are coefficients, not all zero, for which\n\n$$c_1\\vec v_1+\\cdots+c_k\\vec v_k=\\vec0.$$\n\nGeometrically, two nonzero vectors are dependent when they are parallel. Three vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$ are dependent when they all lie in a plane through 